  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/d9482b116bad201dec2c71f173bf18d7dc2f170acbb37052151bba38a7898450.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(5)^{2} & =2(0)+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{25}{2} & =0+0+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{25}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+4(2)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+8+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35} \\\\  & \u22485.9\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
